  "raw_text": "Step 1: Identify all possible ways to insert parentheses into the expression \\(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1\\).\n\nStep 2: Consider the expression without any parentheses: \\(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1 = 121\\).\n\nStep 3: Insert parentheses around different parts of the multiplication and evaluate:\n\n- \\((2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (4 \\cdot 5) + 1 = 6 \\cdot 20 + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121\\)\n- \\((2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5 + 1 = 24 \\cdot 5 + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121\\)\n- \\((2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5) + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121\\)\n- \\(2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5) + 1 = 2 \\cdot 60 + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121\\)\n- \\(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (4 \\cdot 5) + 1 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 20 + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121\\)\n- \\(2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5 + 1 = 2 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 5 + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121\\)\n- \\((2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1 = 6 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121\\)\n- \\((2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4) \\cdot (5 + 1) = 24 \\cdot 6 = 144\\)\n\nStep 4: Evaluate the expression with parentheses around the addition part:\n\n- \\(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot (5 + 1) = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 = 144\\)\n\nStep 5: Check if there are any other unique values by considering different groupings, but no new unique values are found beyond those already listed.\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{8}<|im_end|>",
  "reforward_peak_bytes": 15543297024,
